# Dependencies
# No trailing slash: `node_modules/` matches directories only, and a worktree
# wired with `scripts/setup-worktree.sh --link` has a *symlinked* root
# node_modules, which git then reports as an untracked file (issue #255).
node_modules
.pnpm-store/

# Build output
dist/
build/
.next/
out/
.turbo/
storybook-static/

# Test & coverage
coverage/
.nyc_output/
*.lcov

# `perf:storefront`'s raw Lighthouse reports. `lhci` writes them to the
# directory it was invoked in; `scripts/perf-storefront.sh` deletes the
# directory before every run and copies what it produced under
# `storefront/test-results/`, which is where the CI job picks its artifact up.
.lighthouseci/

# Logs
*.log
npm-debug.log*
pnpm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
lerna-debug.log*

# Tailwind source guard (feature 080 T044; feature 110 T125). The admin and
# storefront suites write a probe carrying an arbitrary-value utility into each
# package they compose, compile the app's stylesheet and assert it survived.
#
# There were two rules here ignoring `**/__tailwind-source-probe__*.{ts,tsx}`,
# and they are **deleted rather than moved**: Tailwind consults `.gitignore` for
# the descendants of an `@source` base, so a probe this repository ignores is a
# probe the scanner never reads. That was harmless only while the admin reached
# packages through one glob pattern; the moment a package declared its own
# directory sources (`admin-stylesheet-composition.md` R1.2) every source-side
# probe read as unscanned. Measured on this tree: an identical file under a
# non-ignored name is emitted, the ignored one is not — so the rule that existed
# to keep a killed run tidy was, under the new spelling, the thing that would
# have made this guard assert nothing at all.
#
# The probes are removed in a `finally`, and a leftover is now something
# `git status` shows. That is the better failure: a file a reviewer can see
# beats a guard nobody can trust.

